<div><span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;"><font color="#0f172a">	</font></span></div><div><font color="#0f172a">After transferral to the P&amp;R ward for intensive rehabilitation, physical therapy and occupational therapy were initiated. Laboratory data on 06/29 revealed anemia (Hb: 9.6 g/dL, Ht: 26.6%), hyponatremia (Na: 131 mEq/L), and hypokalemia (K: 3.1 mEq/L). C-spine and T-L spine X-rays showed degenerative cervical changes with C5-6 disc narrowing, a thoracic compression fracture at T9, post-operative changes at T7, T8, T10, and T11, and status post vertebroplasty at L2. The surgical wound healed well, and all staples were removed on 06/29. The central venous catheter was also removed following the completion of the Cerebrolysin therapy course on 06/29. For myofascial pain management, Clonazepam 0.5 mg 1 tablet QD PO was added. As her wound pain subsided, Oxycodone HCL 5 mg TID PRN PO was discontinued; additionally, the combination of Chlorzoxazone 250 mg/Acetaminophen 300 mg TID was switched to Acetaminophen 500 mg 1 tablet Q6H PO. For poor sleep, Zopiclone 7.5 mg 0.5 tablet HS PRN PO was prescribed. To address her mild depression and poor motivation, an oncology psychologist was consulted. Milder left neck torticollis and myofascial pain were noted, though PRN Morphine was still required occasionally due to unspecified pain and insomnia.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp; Regarding motor function, physical therapy focused on muscle strengthening exercises, active range of motion exercises, therapeutic exercises (including mat and bed exercises), neuromuscular facilitation to address insufficient movement, and sitting/standing balance training. Furthermore, general weakness with poor endurance, as well as dizziness and vomiting following physical activity, were noted, with orthostatic hypotension highly suspected. Currently, she tolerated wheelchair sitting for up to 30 minutes. She achieved a standing posture with a walker for 20 seconds, while performing sit-to-stand transitions required minimal to moderate assistance.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp; Cognitive attention, emotional support, and functional interventions were provided during occupational therapy, with sessions focusing on sitting endurance training, sitting/standing balance training (including sitting on the edge of the bed, sit-to-stand practice with a walker, and marching in place), upper extremity functional training, and lower extremity AROM/ankle pumping to enhance strength and endurance. Spinal orthosis (back brace) application training was also incorporated. In her activities of daily living, she demonstrated functional progression through targeted training. She improved from requiring maximal assistance to achieving modified independence in feeding. For other essential tasks, she progressed to or maintained a level of modified independence in grooming, and required minimal assistance for bed mobility, dressing, toileting, and transfers.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp; On 07/08, new-onset left peripheral facial palsy and tongue deviation to the left were detected, accompanied by family reports of drowsiness, poor mental status, and reduced oral intake. Her consciousness level remained at baseline with a GCS of E4M6V5, and she was able to follow commands. Neurological examination showed left facial palsy and tongue deviation to the left, but extraocular movements (EOM) were intact without nystagmus or diplopia. Pupils were 3/3 mm with prompt light reflex. Muscle strength was scored as follows: RUE (P/D): 4/4+, LUE (P/D): 4+/4+, RLE (P/D): 3+/4, and LLE (P/D): 4+/4+.Regarding acute management, normal saline hydration was given, and Prednisolone was administered as suggested by the neurologist. Follow-up lab data showed persistent mild hyponatremia (131 → 130 mEq/L) and an elevation in GOT (31 → 42 U/L). Brain CT revealed no cerebral hemorrhage but showed multiple metastatic skull lesions and a left cerebellar lesion with associated vasogenic edema. A brain MRI was arranged for further evaluation. The radiologist reviewed the brain CT results and advised deferring the discussion of definitive subsequent treatment pending the brain MRI findings. Meanwhile, Megest oral suspension and Bfluid were administered to address her poor appetite and nutritional status. For pain control, Oxycodone was resumed, and oral Morphine was added. Due to advanced ovarian cancer with distant metastases and new acute intracranial lesions, she was transferred to the GYN ward for further oncology management.</font></div><div></div>